BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 344.00 Td (Why do vector databases need indexing? Every time a user asks a question, we encode that query into an) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 330.60 Td (embedding vector on the fly. To answer the question, we need to find the most semantically similar vectors) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 317.20 Td (\(chunks\) from our stored corpus. If we naively compare the query embedding with every stored embedding one) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 303.80 Td (by one, it becomes very slow as the number of chunks grows.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 283.90 Td (Vector indexes solve this by organizing embeddings in a special data structure \(HNSW graphs, IVFFlat, etc.\) so) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 270.50 Td (that we can jump directly to the most promising neighbors instead of scanning everything. That\222s what makes) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 257.10 Td (similarity search fast enough to be usable in production RAG systems. I can talk about different indexing) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 243.70 Td (techniques in a different article, the better the indexing strategy, the better and faster the retrieval would be. By) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 230.30 Td (default, all of these databases come up with these indexing strategies where we can choose.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 210.40 Td (For clarity, the query embeddings are not stored anywhere; they are just calculated on the fly, just the document) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 197.00 Td (chunks\222 embeddings are stored in the Vector DB. The same model must be used for both the document and) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 183.60 Td (query embedding to ensure uniformity between the two.) Tj ET
BT /F2 10.5 Tf 58.00 152.70 Td (Retrieval) Tj ET
