BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 695.00 Td (Parameterized queries not only improve security but also allow for better caching and performance optimization.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 675.10 Td (Handling Scale with Connection Pooling) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 655.20 Td (If your app suddenly goes viral and thousands of users start querying at once, creating a new database) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 641.80 Td (connection for every request would slow things down fast.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 621.90 Td (That\222s where connection pooling comes in. Supabase uses pgBouncer, a lightweight PostgreSQL connection) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 608.50 Td (pooler, which keeps a pool of open connections ready to be reused. This ensures queries are handled efficiently) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 595.10 Td (no matter how much traffic spikes.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 575.20 Td (Thanks for reading LevelYouUp! Subscribe for free to receive new posts and support my work.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 555.30 Td (Enforcing Security with Row-Level Security \(RLS\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 535.40 Td (Here\222s where things get interesting.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 515.50 Td (Now, before PostgREST sends the query to PostgreSQL for it to execute, there is one more important layer that) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 502.10 Td (comes in, and that is Row Level Security. Now what\222s this new thing that everyone\222s talking about?) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 482.20 Td (The thing is, PostgREST first has to make sure that whoever is executing this query is actually allowed to read) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 468.80 Td (the data or not.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 448.90 Td (And as we discussed, BaaS does not have a backend as we tend to connect the frontend directly to Supabase.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 435.50 Td (If you don\222t have any backend protection layer, what if data is not secure or gets leaked, especially when you try) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 422.10 Td (to talk to the DB directly from the frontend? That\222s where RLS comes in.) Tj ET
