BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 694.75 Td (knowledge distillation or model distillation is the process of transferring knowledge from a large model to a) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 681.35 Td (smaller one) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 661.45 Td (We already have a huge, smart model that's fully trained. But it's expensive at scale \(costs a lot in compute and) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 648.05 Td (energy\), needs heavy hardware, and can't fit on small devices, so for everyday use, we actually want a small,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 634.65 Td (fast model: one that uses less compute and memory, responds quickly, and can run on smaller devices. Instead) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 621.25 Td (of training that small model from scratch on raw data, we have it copy the big model's predictions. This is) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 607.85 Td (knowledge distillation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 587.95 Td (Google's Gemma models are distilled from Gemini. Meta's Llama 4 Scout and Maverick are distilled from the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 574.55 Td (2-trillion-parameter Behemoth model.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 554.65 Td (In 2006 published paper Model Compression, At the time, models were tiny by today's standards \(maybe 500MB) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 541.25 Td (to 1GB\), but the problem then was that the best performing models weren't single models at all. They were) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 527.85 Td (ensembles : collections of hundreds or thousands of independently trained classifiers, whose predictions were) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 514.45 Td (averaged together for a final answer.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 494.55 Td (Ensembles worked well because training back then was noisy and sensitive to random initialization. Train a) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 481.15 Td (thousand slightly different models on the same spam-detection task, and some come out great, some come out) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 467.75 Td (mediocre. Averaging all of them would give better results.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 447.85 Td (The problem back then was that a thousand-model ensemble cant fit into a device with limited storage. So the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 434.45 Td (Cornell paper proposed something clever: instead of shipping the whole ensemble, train one small model to) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 421.05 Td (mimic the ensemble's averaged output) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 401.15 Td (The small model was not trained only on the original hard labels from the dataset, such as \223spam\224 or \223not) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 387.75 Td (spam.\224 Instead, it was trained to match the ensemble\222s averaged probability output for example, $$) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 374.35 Td (P\(\\text{spam}\) = 0.73 $$ rather than simply $$ \\text{spam} = 1 $$ These soft labels contain more information) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 360.95 Td (about the ensemble\222s behavior, including how confident it is in each prediction. By learning these probability) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 347.55 Td (outputs, the small model is learning the behavior. This soft, probability-based target is what makes the whole) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 334.15 Td (idea work, and it's the seed of everything that came after) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 314.25 Td (The 2006 idea sat mostly unused until 2015, Distilling the Knowledge in a Neural Network at Google. This paper) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 300.85 Td (is where the field got its name and its "teacher/student" terminology. It also made a discovery that goes beyond) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 287.45 Td (the 2006 work: you don't need an ensemble at all. A single well-trained teacher model is enough. If you already) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 274.05 Td (have one big classifier and want to compress it, you're still better off training the small student on the teacher's) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 260.65 Td (soft probability output than on the dataset's hard labels.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 240.75 Td (They tested this on MNIST, the standard handwritten-digit dataset \(0\2269\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 220.85 Td (Take a messy handwritten "5." A hard label says "5 = 100%, everything else = 0%," discarding any resemblance) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 207.45 Td (to other digits. The teacher's actual output before rounding to a final answer might look like: 5 -> 60%, 3 -> 30%, 8) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 194.05 Td (-> 10%.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 174.15 Td (That 30% on "3" is information: it tells the student that this input resembles a 3 more than it resembles any other) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 160.75 Td (non-5 digit. So this paper called this dark knowledge: information about what an input is not, contained in the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 147.35 Td (small soft non-zero probabilities that a hard label erases. The teacher has already worked out these) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 133.95 Td (resemblances; the student only has to copy them, which is why it can use far fewer parameters than the teacher) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 120.55 Td (and still perform well.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 100.65 Td (This distillation process can happen pre-training, or post-training or both. Llamma does it during pre-training.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 80.75 Td (Google and Meta does Proper distillation - type of knowledge distillation) Tj ET
