BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 694.75 Td (We saw the 2 problems in scaling neural networks in this post) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 674.85 Td (Distributed Data Parallelism solves scaling when data is the bottleneck, but it assumes the whole model fits on) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 661.45 Td (one GPU.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 641.55 Td (But what if the model itself is too big to fit ?) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 621.65 Td (Thats where Model Parallelism comes in.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 601.75 Td (For scenarios where the model is too large to fit on a single GPU, we need model parallelism to distribute the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 588.35 Td (parameters across multiple GPU devices.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 568.45 Td (There are 2 main ways the models can be distributed :) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 548.55 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 548.55 Td (we can either distribute the various layers / blocks across different GPUs \(pipeline parallelism\) eg : GPU-1) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 535.15 Td (has layers 1\2265, GPU-2 has layers 6-10, data flows sequentially through GPUs, one layer's output feeds the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 521.75 Td (next GPU.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 72.00 505.85 Td (\225) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 505.85 Td (split individual layers themselves across GPUs \(tensor parallelism\) meaning instead of dividing the neural) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 492.45 Td (network by layer \(horizontally\), you divide the tensors themselves \(vertically\). E.g., GPU-1 has half the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 479.05 Td (weight matrix of _one_ layer, GPU-2 has the other half of the same layer, both GPUs work on the same) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 80.01 465.65 Td (layer simultaneously, each computing a slice of the weight matrix.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 449.75 Td (Note Model Parallelism doesn't imply that the training happens in parallel!) Tj ET
