BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 694.75 Td (This is a very common problem when dealing with neural networks.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 674.85 Td (When dealing with Sigmoid activation function activation function, one of the drawbacks is that it has vanishing) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 661.45 Td (gradient effect.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 641.55 Td (As we all know when a neural netowork learns, it works backwards \(backward propagation\) from output to input) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 628.15 Td (layer by layer. And during its backward propagation it learns the weights, parameters etc. based on the loss /) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 614.75 Td (gradient right.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 594.85 Td (Derivative of the Logits function:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 574.95 Td ($$ \\text{logit}\(p\) = \\log\\left\(\\frac{p}{1-p}\\right\) = \\log\(p\) - \\log\(1-p\) $$) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 555.05 Td (Differentiating term by term:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 535.15 Td ($$ \\frac{d}{dp}\\,\\text{logit}\(p\) = \\frac{1}{p} + \\frac{1}{1-p} = \\frac{\(1-p\) + p}{p\(1-p\)} = \\frac{1}{p\(1-p\)} $$) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 515.25 Td (Derivative of the sigmoid function:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 495.35 Td ($$ p =\\sigma\(z\) = \\frac{1}{1+e^{-z}} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\sigma'\(z\) = \\sigma\(z\)\\big\(1-\\sigma\(z\)\\big\) $$ Since) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 481.95 Td ($\\sigma\(z\) = p$, we can write this more simply as:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 462.05 Td ($$ \\sigma'\(z\) = p\(1-p\) $$) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 442.15 Td (As, we found:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 422.25 Td ($$ \\frac{d}{dp}\\,\\text{logit}\(p\) = \\frac{1}{p\(1-p\)} $$) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 402.35 Td (Compare that to what we just got:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 382.45 Td ($$ \\sigma'\(z\) = p\(1-p\) $$) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 362.55 Td (These two are exact reciprocals of each other one is $p\(1-p\)$, the other is $\\frac{1}{p\(1-p\)}$. This isn't a) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 349.15 Td (coincidence, and it isn't specific to logit and sigmoid, it's a general calculus rule that applies to any pair of inverse) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 335.75 Td (functions.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 315.85 Td (Now, when backprop works its way back to a weight $w$, it's not computing $\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w}$ directly) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 302.45 Td (in one shot it breaks it apart using the chain rule, since $w$ affects the loss only through a chain of intermediate) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 289.05 Td (steps \($w \\to z \\to p \\to L$\) because :) Tj ET
